ctlsock: the open-loop belief must integrate counts * gain EXACTLY -- rounding
a fixed-size pacer chunk is a ramp, not a wash

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-home-drain.patch (same file, same branch, last of
the apple2e pointer chain: ptr-tags -> move-step-cap -> open-loop-gain ->
home-drain -> count-carry).

THE BUG. With home-drain in place a FRESH session lands five MOVEA targets
within +-7 px on the apple2e Dazzle Draw canvas. A SECOND lap of the same
five targets in the same session, with no re-home in between, did not:

	target        err x   err y
	(300,200)       +5      +4
	(700,500)      +13      +8
	(150,600)       +4     +12
	(850,150)       +6     +14
	(300,200)       -4     +12

Every Y error is positive and every X error but the last is too. That is a
bias, not jitter, and an open loop has nothing to correct it with: its only
origin is its own belief.

move_rel() advanced the belief by llround(counts * gain) per issued chunk.
The pacer hands out chunks of a FIXED size -- m_move_step, 120 counts here --
so the same residue is dropped every window: 120 * 1.547 = 185.64 px booked
as 186, +0.36 px of phantom travel per window, in whichever direction the
move went. Over a lap of ~3000 px that is a handful of pixels, always the
same sign per direction of travel. Rounding is only a wash when the operands
vary; a pacer exists precisely to make them not vary.

THE FIX. m_bx_frac / m_by_frac: the belief integrates counts * gain in
floating point and keeps the sub-pixel part, so the integer belief never sits
more than 1 px from the exact sum however the travel was chunked. A clamp
drops the carry -- past an edge the guest is not where the arithmetic says it
is. reset_open_loop_carry() zeroes it wherever the belief is re-anchored
rather than integrated: the home slam, the release of the held first target
(the guest IS at the clamp once the slam has drained, so the belief is set to
0,0 there instead of keeping whatever it accumulated on the way), and
reseed_after_restore().

AND THE FIX THAT IS NOT ONE -- MEASURED, so nobody re-adds it. The obvious
companion is a second carry on the OTHER conversion, px_to_counts(): bank the
sub-count remainder of Delta-px / gain and fold it into the next move. It
makes things WORSE, and it reverses the sign of the drift. Built and run,
three laps of the same five targets in one session, X error:

	lap 1   -3   -9   -9  -18
	lap 2  -18  -24  -20  -26
	lap 3  -29  -38  -34  -40

Monotone, ~11 px of drift per lap, against a worst of +13 before the change.
The reason is that the belief integrates the counts ACTUALLY ISSUED: the
rounding residue of px_to_counts() is therefore already visible to the next
MOVEA, as a Delta-px the belief has not covered, and correcting it again in
px_to_counts() applies it twice. Two carries compose only when the second
one's error is invisible to the first. Here it is not, and the doubled
correction is bounded by the +-1-count clamp on the carry, which is why the
error walks off at a steady rate instead of exploding.

WHAT THE FIX BUYS, AND WHAT IT UNCOVERS. MEASURED with this patch, three
laps of the five targets in ONE session, golden copy restored, arrow tip
located in the framebuffer (x>=10, 72<=y<640):

	lap  target      tip        err
	 1   (300,200)  (302,200)   +2, +0
	 1   (700,500)  (706,500)   +6, +0
	 1   (150,600)  (150,604)    0, +4
	 1   (850,150)  (852,152)   +2, +2
	 1   (300,200)  (294,200)   -6, +0
	 2   (300,200)  (294,200)   -6, +0    (no travel: same target)
	 2   (700,500)  (702,504)   +2, +4
	 2   (150,600)  (143,612)   -7, +12
	 2   (850,150)  (834,164)  -16, +14
	 2   (300,200)  (276,212)  -24, +12
	 3   (700,500)  (677,516)  -23, +16
	 3   (150,600)  (117,620)  -33, +20
	 3   (850,150)  (819,164)  -31, +14
	 3   (300,200)  (261,208)  -39, +8

LAP 1 IS THE BEST THIS STATION HAS EVER MEASURED -- worst 6 px, inside the
bar a published pointer contract needs. Laps 2 and 3 are not: from the
seventh move on the landings walk off toward the bottom-left at a steady
rate, ~15 px per lap on X. That drift is NOT arithmetic. The belief now
integrates exactly, STAT still reads bel=300,200 at the end, and the drift
has a FIXED DIRECTION (left and down) rather than a fixed sign per direction
of travel -- so it survives a closed loop of targets whose net displacement is
zero, which no gain error and no rounding residue can do. It is the guest
losing counts, and the suspect is the same 8-bit differencing window that
move-step-cap addressed: the cap guarantees one WRITE never exceeds 127
counts, but two paced writes that land between the same pair of MCU port-B
reads sum at the device, and 240 counts folded into +-0x80 is read as -16.

That also explains why the OLD rounding bias looked better on lap 2 (worst
14 px, all positive): it was a positive error partly cancelling this negative
one. Removing it did not create the drift, it uncovered it -- which is why
this patch is the right change to keep even though the multi-lap numbers got
worse before they get better. The next measurement is a merge accounting at
the device, not another correction in the module.

AND ONE KNOB FOR WHAT NO CARRY CAN FIX. MAME_CTL_REHOME_PX (default 0, off)
re-homes silently before the next MOVEA once the pointer has travelled that
many px since the last home AND the device has had HOME_SETTLE of quiet with
an empty queue. The carry removes the drift the open loop MAKES; it can do
nothing about drift the loop never saw -- a gain seed a little off, a window
the device merged, a guest that moved its own pointer. Re-anchoring against
the one position the guest always agrees about is the open loop's only
recovery. It is off by default because it costs a visible slam.

